<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Tarquinii</h1><p class="st-meta">900 BCE – 281 BCE (approx.) · capital Tarquinii</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Etruscan city famed for its painted tombs and remembered by Rome as the home of the Tarquin dynasty.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tarquinii, Etruscan Tarchna, grew on a plateau above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marta-river">Marta</a> valley near the coast, one of the earliest and largest Villanovan centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a>. In legend it was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarchon">Tarchon</a>, and the haruspex <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tages">Tages</a>, who revealed Etruscan religious lore, appeared in its fields. Its port at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gravisca">Gravisca</a> welcomed Greek traders from the 6th century BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Aristocratic families dominated the city and are commemorated in the painted chamber tombs of the Monterozzi necropolis, more than 200 of them, showing banquets, dancers, athletes and later underworld scenes. Its magistrates bore the title zilath, and the Spurinna family's elogia record their deeds. Roman tradition said <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demaratus-of-corinth">Demaratus of Corinth</a> settled here and his son became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome's</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarquinius-priscus">Tarquinius Priscus</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Tarquinii fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the 4th century BCE, notoriously sacrificing 307 Roman prisoners in 358 BCE. After further wars it made peace and became a Roman ally around 281 BCE, and later a municipium. Its painted tombs are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tarquinii.jpg" alt="Tarquinii" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: AlexanderVanLoon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tarquinia_town_square_AvL.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 BCE – 281 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tarquinii</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Etruscan religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Etruscan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tarquinia, Tarchna</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-590&amp;focus=tarquinii" aria-label="Tarquinii on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4965 675 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tarquinii lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5285 931L5290 918M5285 931L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5290 918h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5285 931h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5285 931h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-590&amp;focus=tarquinii">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

